Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:58:59 +0000

lab-process-mail answers one message and exits, so new mail waits
until the operator runs it again by hand. lab-process-maild keeps it
answered in the background, and the operator only types start, stop,
or status.

lab-process-mail stays a one-shot program and lab-process-maild only
manages it as a service. daemon(8) already detaches, keeps a log and a
pidfile, and reruns its child after a delay, so the script hands its
own drain command to daemon(8) with -R rather than sleeping and
waiting on children itself. daemon(8) cannot act on its child's exit
status, so drain is the one loop: it runs lab-process-mail again at
once while it exits 75, so a batch of mail is answered back to back
and the interval applies only once the inbox is empty or a run fails.
Because sh holds a trapped SIGTERM until the foreground command ends,
stop lets the run in progress finish without drain putting anything
in the background.

It is configured the way an rc(8) service is. The package installs
under a LOCALBASE, such as ~/local, but the log and pidfile belong to
the host, so each has its own setting in an rc.conf(5)-style file
under LOCALBASE/etc. Their defaults are /var/log and /var/run for root
and ~/log and ~/var/run for anyone else, so a user install works with
no conf at all.

daemon(8) starts from an empty environment plus what
lab_process_maild_env names, so how the service behaves does not
depend on which shell happened to start it. PATH is kept as the
operator has it, and the conf is the one place to change it.

lab-process-maild(8) documents the commands and every setting.
